<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Drangiana</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Zarangiana</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Drangiana was an ancient region in the basin of the Helmand river and Lake Hamun, on the borders of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>. It was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satrapy</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a> and was crossed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> in 330 BCE. After Saka groups settled there in the late second century BCE it became known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sistan">Sakastan</a>, the origin of the modern name Sistan.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/drangiana.jpg" alt="Drangiana" width="320" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mossmaps · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eastern_Satrapies_of_the_Achaemenid_Empire.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After their displacement in the second century BCE, Saka groups moved into Bactria, into <b>Drangiana</b>, which took the name Sakastan (modern Sistan), and through the mountains into Gandhara and north-western India.</blockquote><a href="/empire/saka">Saka · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
